\begin_layout Standard
These phenomena manifest themselves in the form of optical selection rules
 in semiconducting TMDC monolayers (although not present in bilayers or
 thicker).
 As with normal semiconductors, charge carriers can be promoted from the
 valence band to the conduction band.
 However, the spin states in each band are different for the K and -K valleys
 and can be selectively excited using left- and right-handed circularly
 polarised light.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "cao2012valleyselective,kioseoglou2016optical"

\end_inset

 This selectivity arises from angular momentum conservation and time reversal
 symmetry constraints.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "xu2014spinand"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another attractive feature of semiconducting TMDCs is their exotic exciton
 physics.
 Promotion of carriers in monolayer semiconducting TMDCs cause electron
 hole pairs to form strongly bound exciton states due to the enhanced Coulomb
 interaction between the carriers that results from 2D confinement.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "klots2014probing,poellmann2015resonant"

\end_inset

 These unique properties of semiconducting TMDCs allow a plethora of physical
 phenomena to be explored
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "xiao2012coupled,mak2014thevalley,newaz2013electrical"

\end_inset

 and ultimately find application in ultrasensitive photodetectors
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lopez-sanchez2013ultrasensitive,tsai2013fewlayer"

\end_inset

 and energy harvesting applications
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bernardi2013extraordinary"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second class of 2D TMDCs are metallic: niobium diselenide, niobium disulfide
, tantallum diselenide and tantallum disulfide, although their band structure
 can potentially be made semiconducting if formed into nanoribbons.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "li2013metalto"

\end_inset

 Niobium diselenide is of particular interest currently as, if cooled sufficient
ly, charge density waves are observed.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wilson1975chargedensity"

\end_inset

 These take the form of an ordered fluid of electrons that flow in a highly
 correlated but 'bumpy' manner through a material, and is thought to be
 linked to an unconventional form of superconductivity in layered materials.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "xi2015strongly"

\end_inset

 Their occurance in 2D materials has not been fully explained theoretically
 and so they are of great interest to fundamental research in condensed
 matter physics.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "chatterjee2015emergence,ugeda2016characterization"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Bilayer (and thicker) TMDCs are capable of forming different stable structural
 polytypes (stacking orders) which often lead to modification of the electronic
 properties.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "voiry2015phaseengineering"

\end_inset

 Furthermore, within these polytypes different polymorphs of the monolayers
 are possible i.e.
 where the metal atom is trigonally or octahedrally coordinated.
 Isolated monolayer TMDCs can have different polymorphs (metal atom coordination
) but not polytypes as they are not stacked.
 In thicker TMDCs the most common polytypes are 1T, 2H and 3R, where each
 letter stands for trigonal, hexagonal and rhombohedral, respectively.
 The digits denote the number of monolayers in the unit cell.
 The dominant phase in a TMDC crystal is largely down to how the crystal
 was synthesised and its disposition towards a polymorph.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "shmeliov2014unusual,voiry2015phaseengineering"

\end_inset

 For example, MoS
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 is most stable in the 2H phase with the metal atom trigonally coordinated,
 whereas TiS
\begin_inset script subscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2 
\end_layout

\end_inset

takes the 1T phase where the metal is octohedrally coordinated.
 Multiple phases can even coexist within a flake and can arise during synthesis
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "enyashin2013linedefects,lee2016ramansignatures"

\end_inset

 or patterned using electron beam irradiation
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lin2014atomicmechanism"

\end_inset

.
 Finally, azimuthal rotation of monolayers relative to one another creates
 variations in stacking via a moiré effect but does not seem to yield distinct
 and different polytypes 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "puretzky2016twisted"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Potential applications of graphene
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In addition to graphene's incredible electronic merits are a host of other
 properties which are unrivalled by other materials.
 It is the thinnest imaginable material (one atom thick)
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2004electric"

\end_inset

; nearly completely optically tranparent
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "nair2008finestructure"

\end_inset

, has the largest specific surface area (2630 m
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
2
\end_layout

\end_inset

 g
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

)
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bonaccorso2015graphene"

\end_inset

; the strongest and stiffest material ever measured (Young's modulus of
 1 TPa and intrinsic strength of 130 GPa)
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lee2008measurement"

\end_inset

; has the highest thermal conductivity (> 3,000 W mK
\begin_inset script superscript

\begin_layout Plain Layout
-1
\end_layout

\end_inset

)
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "balandin2011thermal"

\end_inset

; the highest tolerance of electronic current density (a million times that
 of copper)
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "moser2007currentinduced,murali2009breakdown"

\end_inset

 and longest mean free path of electrons under ambient conditions
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "tse2008ballistic"

\end_inset

; the most stretchable crystal
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "guinea2010energygaps"

\end_inset

 and is impermiable to gases
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bunch2008impermeable"

\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These myriad superior properties (most of which are only observed in the
 highest quality flakes) make graphene an ideal candidate for many applications.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2012aroadmap,ferrari2015science"

\end_inset

 Graphene-based inks and paints are expected to provide conductive inks,
 antistatic and interference shielding, corrosion barriers and gas membranes.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "kirkland2012exploring,yoo2014graphene,su2014impermeable,wang2015electrical,cui2016gasbarrier"

\end_inset

 Transparent conductive coatings for consumer flexible electronics (e.g.
 flexible touch screens and rollable electronic paper) are already being
 researched commercially.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "bae2010rolltoroll,ahn2014graphene,samsung"

\end_inset

 By embedding graphene in polymer or ceramic matrices a new class of superstrong
, lightweight and degradation resistant composites are anticipated to emerge
 over the next ten years.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "kim2010graphenepolymer,verdejo2011graphene,young2012themechanics"

\end_inset

 Graphene based composites are also poised to play important roles in boosting
 the performance of many electrochemical and optoelectronic applications
 such as energy harvesting and storage technologies (fuel cells, supercapacitors
, batteries, etc.).
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "huang2012graphenebased"

\end_inset

 The properties of graphene are extremely sensitive to environment and can
 be used in many different sensor applications.
 These range from drilling holes in graphene in order to sequence DNA
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "feng2015identification,paulechka2016nucleobasefunctionalized"

\end_inset

, to flakes acting as intrinsic strain gauges via Raman spectroscopy characteris
ation
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "neumann2015ramanspectroscopy,delcorro2015strainassessment"

\end_inset

.
 Other sensing applications include gas environment, pressure and magnetic
 field, with devices possibly measuring many of these metrics simultaneously.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chen2012optical,zhao2013infrared,huang2015multifunctional,schedin2007detection"

\end_inset

 Graphene is easily functionalised
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "kuila2012chemical,georgakilas2016noncovalent"

\end_inset

 yet relatively inert which opens up possibilities in bioapplications, particula
rly in biomolecular sensing, tissue engineering, regenerative medicine and
 drug delivery.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "hirtz2013multiplexed,liu2013graphene,goenka2014graphenebased"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It is widely anticipated that graphene will make it's biggest impact in
 the fields of electronics and photonics.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "castroneto2009theelectronic,avouris2010graphene,schwierz2010graphene,bao2012graphene,bonaccorso2010graphene"

\end_inset

 The poignant absence of a band gap in monolayer and bilayer graphene all
 but eliminates it's application as a speedy and direct replacement for
 silicon in conventional logic gate archetectures.
 Nevertheless, there are currently three stratagies to open a band gap in
 graphene: confining the width of graphene to form nanoribbons
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chen2007graphene,li2008chemically"

\end_inset

, biasing bilayer graphene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "castro2007biasedbilayer,ohta2006controlling,zhang2009directobservation"

\end_inset

, and straining graphene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pereira2009tightbinding,ni2008uniaxial"

\end_inset

.
 Monolayer graphene nanoribbons currently show the most promise with bandgaps
 scaling inversely with the width of the ribbon, in some cases reaching
 200 meV for < 20 nm widths
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chen2007graphene,li2008chemically"

\end_inset

.
 However, amongst problems relating to reproducible ribbon quality, confined
 carriers rapidly lose mobility and risk negating any advantages over silicon.
 The main motivation for integrating graphene in logic devices is to extend
 the trend in integrated circuit advancement as professed by Moore's Law,
 which in turn forms the basis of financial growth for the semiconductor
 industry.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "moore2003noexponential,schwierz2010nanometer"

\end_inset

 Through reduction in the size of silicon metal-oxide-semiconductor field-effect
 transitors (MOSFETs) the performance of integrated circuits has roughly
 doubled every 18 months for the last fifty years.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "schwierz2010graphene"

\end_inset

 However size reduction is rapidly approaching it's limit in silicon and
 new materials are being sought to increase device performance and decrease
 feature size.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "itrsnews"

\end_inset

 Although mobility is typically cited as graphene's most attractive feature
 for high speed logic devices, it is actually the potential to have robust
 single atom channel widths which sustains interest within the semiconductor
 community (despite a lack of bandgap).
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "schwierz2010graphene"

\end_inset

 New device concepts are being explored to work around graphene's shortcomings,
 the most promising of which is the bilayer pseudospin FET; two graphene
 monolayers separated by a similarly thin dielectric.
 To switch the device on a gate bias is applied which encourages carriers
 to tunnel through the dielectric, effectively creating a short circuit
 current to flow through the channel.
 Under different bias conditions the tunnelling resistance is high, suppressing
 tunnelling and switching the device off.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "banerjee2009bilayer"

\end_inset

 This is anticipated to yield high on-off ratios with a very low switching
 energy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The unique optical properties of graphene make it an excellent candidate
 material for many photonic devices.
 Photodetectors, mode-locked lasers, polarizers, isolater devices and optical
 modulators are all anticipated applications for graphene's optical properties,
 many of which already have proof of concept devices.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "eberlein2008plasmon,ju2011graphene,kim2011graphenebased,chen2012optical,fei2012gatetuning,grigorenko2012graphene,polat2013broadband,wang2013highresponsivity,yan2013damping,marshall2013electronically,chakraborty2016gainmodulation"

\end_inset

 Optical modulators are key components in photonics systems as they are
 used to control the characteristics of light packets carrying signals between
 devices.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "luo2015graphenebased"

\end_inset

 Modulation speed, the spectral range of the modulator, energy consumption
 and device size all dictate the quality of an optical modulator, and all
 of which depend on modulating material.
 Graphene is particularly useful for optical modulation for three reason:
 Graphene has a high modulation speed owing to it's intrinsically high carrier
 mobility; the absorption and modulation characteristics can be directly
 controlled by changing the Fermi level using a gate bias; and graphene
 has a large optical bandwidth with a constant absorption of 2.293% from
 the infrared to visible wavelengths (which happens to coincide with the
 optical communication bandwidth required).
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "luo2015graphenebased2,polat2013broadband"

\end_inset

 This absorption may seem low, however when taken into consideration with
 graphene's single atom thickness it is in fact very high compared to other
 photonics materials.
 These properties could prove most lucrative in terahertz modulators and
 detectors, which would begin to bridge what has become known as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

terahertz gap
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and pave the way to cheap and powerful long wavelength applications.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ju2011graphene,rahm2012thzwave,marshall2013electronically"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main bottlenecks to progress in all these areas lie in the fundamental
 problems of handling graphene and scaleable synthesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Making and transferring graphene flakes
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Making-and-transferring graphene"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The papers presented in the following sections of this thesis use devices
 made from mechanically exfoliated 2D flakes.
 For context a brief review of other synthesis routes to graphene flakes
 are presented here.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The myriad different methods for synthesising graphene flakes fall broadly
 under mechanical exfoliation, chemical growth and liquid phase exfoliation.
 Exfoliation methods are considered 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

top-down
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 synthesis routes as they directly remove graphene sheets from a bulk crystal
 of graphite.
 Chemical growth methods are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bottom-up
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 as they typically use a carbon-based precursor gas and favourable growth
 conditions (a catalyst, heat, pressure-control etc) to form sheets of graphene
 on a surface.
 The relative merits of each of these methods are best judged in the context
 of their target application.
 For example, liquid exfoliation methods are best suited to inexpensive
 large-volume production of graphene suspensions to generate conductive
 surfaces, composites and barrier films.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "huo20152dmaterials"

\end_inset

 However, liquid exfoliation struggles to make atomically thin, clean, highly
 crystalline, large area flakes required for electronic and photonic devices
 (this is not for lack of trying
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "withers2014heterostructures"

\end_inset

).
 Nevertheless, liquid exfoliation methods are easily the cheapest and most
 scaleable syntheses for graphene (and other 2D materials), and as such
 the applications which use liquid exfoliated graphene will be the first
 to be commercialised.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "novoselov2012aroadmap,ferrari2015science"

\end_inset

 Liquid exfoliation methods generally disperse bulk flakes in solvent and
 use ultrasound to induce bubble cavitation, which applies stresses to the
 bulk flakes causing them to cleave and fragment.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "yi2015areview"

\end_inset

 Different centrifuging methods have shown to be size and shape selective
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "paton2014scalable"

\end_inset

 and other liquid exfoliation strategies also use ion intercalation to cleave
 sheets of graphene.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "nicolosi2013liquidexfoliation,abdelkader2015howto"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The highest quality devices, used for prototyping, proof-of-concept and
 exploring new physical phenomena, are still made from mechanically exfoliatated
 flakes.
 Mechanical exfoliation is highly inefficient in terms of materials and
 time.
 Only a few suitable flakes are produced from a bulk crystal of graphite
 and, whilst the exfoliation step is not very time consuming, manually searching
 for flakes in an optical microscope is.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "yi2015areview"

\end_inset

 Furthermore, the size, shape and crystal directions of graphene flakes
 are not controllable by this method.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "avouris2012graphene"

\end_inset

 Chemical growth methods are already showing great promise for the controlled
 growth of graphene and other 2D materials on different surfaces.
 Chemical growth can be roughly split into two principle methodologies:
 silicon-carbide (SiC) epitaxy and chemical vapour deposition (CVD).
 In the former, a crystalline wafer of SiC is heated in excess of 2000°C
 in an inert atmosphere.
 The silicon atoms at the lowest energy surface sublimate and the remaining
 carbon rearranges to graphene.
 This method offers fine control of graphene thickness on an insulating
 substrate and has been shown to synthesise high quality flakes.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "norimatsu2014epitaxial,norimatsu2014growthof"

\end_inset

 CVD is a very versitile technique as not only does it offer controlled
 deposition of graphene there are also a large variety of surfaces on which
 it can be grown
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "avouris2012graphene"

\end_inset

, the best of which is currently copper for monolayer graphene
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chen2011synthesis,mueller2014growing"

\end_inset

.
 Graphene is either grown from a carbon-based gas precursor on a catalytic
 metal surface, or by surface segregation of carbon precipitating from solution
 in the metal bulk (both mechanisms can coexist, e.g.
 graphene synthesised on nickel in methane
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "huang2012synthesis"

\end_inset

).
 Despite the bottom-up nature of this method there are a few disadvantages
 to overcome before this supplants mechanical exfoliation for device prototyping.
 The first, and biggest problem, is that the electronic properties of a
 flake cannot be tested whilst on a metallic substrate, requiring an additional
 transfer step.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "martins2013directtransfer,zaretski2015processes,chen2016progress"

\end_inset

 The second disadvantage is that multiple nucleation sites on the catalyst
 surface lead to many graphene crystallites growing simultaneously.
 At the end of the reaction, when the catalyst surface is completely covered,
 these crystallites interface with grain boundaries between azimuthially
 rotated graphene grains.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "avouris2012graphene"

\end_inset

 Grain boundaries are thought to disrupt the ballistic transport of carriers
 through graphene and degrade the quality of the electronic properties.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "huang2011grainsand,zhang2014grainboundary"

\end_inset

 More recently, large single crystals (> 0.4 mm across) have been grown under
 very carefully controlled conditions.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "li2011largearea,xu2016ultrafast"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Almost regardless of how graphene is made, poor handling rapidly degrades
 flake quality as crystal defects, wrinkles, tears and impurities set in.
 Indeed, careful handing of CVD grown graphene has electronic properties
 to rival that of mechanically exfoliated graphene.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "banszerus2015ultrahighmobility"

\end_inset

 Handling is most likely the next key hurdle to high quality graphene being
 realised commercially.
 Strategies rely on the differing cohesion between materials, with transfer
 techniques to and from different surfaces developed to aid the rapid but
 controlled transfer of graphene sheets from synthesis to target surfaces.
 The majority of these techniques are developed for SiC epataxially or CVD
 grown graphene.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kang2012graphene,zaretski2015processes,chen2016progress"

\end_inset

 Nevertheless, many of the transfer techniques developed for CVD grown 2D
 materials are possible candidate transfer techniques for fabricating van
 der Waals' heterostructures.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "banszerus2015ultrahighmobility"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the following sections the two most popular transfer methods (at time
 of writing) are described: wet and dry transfer techniques.
 These are used in to fabricate the heterostructures presented in the following
 chapters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
van der Waals heterostructure fabrication
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two popular methods used to transfer one mechanically exfoliated
 2D crystal on top of another: dry transfer and wet transfer, although other
 techniques are possible for different synthesis routes and may replace
 these soon.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chen2016progress"

\end_inset

 Wet transfer uses a sacrificial water soluble polymer layer to transfer
 2D flakes.
 The sacrificial layer is spin coated onto a silicon substrate and then
 further coated with polymethyl-meth-acrylate (PMMA).
 Graphite is mechanically exfoliated onto this polymer stack and thin flakes
 identified by optical microscopy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "blake2007makinggraphene"

\end_inset

 and Raman spectroscopy
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "ferrari2006ramanspectrum"

\end_inset

.
 To isolate a suitable flake, the stack is floated on the surface of a water
 bath until the water soluble polymer dissolves, allowing the silicon substrate
 to sink.
 A glass slide with a cut hole in the middle is then manouvered with a micromani
pulator over the flake on the floating PMMA to remove them from the bath,
 all observed under an optical microscope.
 The PMMA is flipped over so that the flake-side is facing the bench.
 The flake is aligned above a target substrate flake (e.g.
 hBN) and the two brought into contact to make a 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

stack
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 The PMMA is then dissolved with acetone and washed away.
 The stack is annealled to > 110 °C to drive off solvents and contamination.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Dry transfer is a variation on the wet transfer technique which attempts
 to minimise contamination between flakes and greatly speed up the transfer
 process.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "withers2015lightemitting,wang2013onedimensional,kretinin2014electronic"

\end_inset

 The wet transfer procedure is followed to the point where a flake is isolated
 on a PMMA membrane above a smaller target flake.
 Once the two flakes are in contact, instead of dissolving the PMMA, the
 PMMA is lifted from the substrate bringing the two flakes with it.
 This step is then repeated for many flakes to build up a bespoke complex
 stack of different 2D materials.
 On the last transfer step, the PMMA is dissolved with acetone and the stack
 annealled.
 Through this method the majority of interfaces between flakes will be isolated
 from PMMA and solvents, greatly reducing the PMMA residue present.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kang2012graphene,wang2013onedimensional,banszerus2015ultrahighmobility"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Stacks of 2D crystals are converted into devices by adding metallic contacts
 to the surface
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "britnell2013resonant,gorbachev2007weaklocalization,gorbachev2014detecting"

\end_inset

 or edges of graphene sheets
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang2013onedimensional,kretinin2014electronic"

\end_inset

.
 More recently, in the latest generation of devices, metal contacts are
 placed on the surface of an encapsulating hBN layer so that electrons can
 be injected by tunnelling into buried conductive layers or used as a gate
 to probe the electronic properties of a device.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "britnell2012electron,britnell2012fieldeffect,britnell2013stronglightmatter,georgiou2013vertical,withers2014heterostructures,cao2015quality"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
van der Waals' heterostructures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Band structure (or band gap) engineering of semiconductors underpins modern
 electronics
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "thayne2009reviewof,thenobel"

\end_inset

, and has lead to breakthrough applications such as high mobility MOSFET
 transistors and light emitting diodes (LEDs)
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "the2014"

\end_inset

.
 Band structure engineering is traditionally achieved by growing different
 semiconductor crystals sequentially to form an extended layered superlattice.
 When a charge carrier travels from one crystal to another, it is influenced
 by the host crystal's band structure.
 This effect can be used to create quantum wells, where electrons and holes
 become confined within a small region of the superlattice where the band
 gap is smallest.
 Confinement of carriers allows exotic physical phenomena to be explored,
 for example exciton states and photoluminescence.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pkbhattacharya1993quantum"

\end_inset

 The fabrication of these superlattices is not trivial.
 For example, growth methods for traditional heterostructures use expensive
 bottom-up techniques such as molecular beam epitaxy (MBE) and pulsed laser
 deposition which require high vacuum conditions
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "chang1985molecular"

\end_inset

.
 Furthermore, the choice of materials is severely limited; the semiconductor
 crystals need to have very similar lattice constants as the layers are
 strongly bound to one another, however the electronic properties can be
 fine tuned with doping.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "gerthsen1995structural,xie2016modular"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In contrast, van der Waals' heterostructures fabricated from mechanical
 exfoliated flakes are comparitively straightforward.
 The different 2D materials do not need to have matching lattice constants
 due to their top-down assembly
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "geim2013vander"

\end_inset

; the 2D flakes can be randomly orientated to one another and still produce
 interesting band structures
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "kretinin2014electronic,withers2015lightemitting"

\end_inset

, or they can be aligned with high angular precision to explore exotic physical
 phenomena
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "dean2013hofstadters,ponomarenko2013cloning,woods2014commensurateincommensurate"

\end_inset

; the handling of the majority of 2D materials does not require epitaxy
 methods within a complex high vacuum system
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lin2014directsynthesis,avouris2012graphene,li2015hexagonal,sutter2013scalable,woods2016onestep"

\end_inset

; and those 2D materials which are sensitive to air can be encapsulated
 within other 2D materials to produce inert heterostuctures with novel propertie
s
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "cao2015quality,mishchenko2015nonlocal,cheng2016vander"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The unprecidented control over the handling and characterisation of 2D materials
 within van der Waals' heterostructures has lead to them being used in conjuncti
on with other materials such as nanomaterials
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "konstantatos2012hybridgraphenequantum,jariwala2013gatetunable,withers2014heterostructures"

\end_inset

, traditional semiconductor materials
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "chung2010transferable,yang2012graphene,lopez-sanchez2014lightgeneration,cheng2016vander"

\end_inset

, self-assembled monolayers
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang2009roomtemperature,lee2014epitaxial,he2014twodimensional"

\end_inset

, and even biological molecules
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "loan2014graphenemos2"

\end_inset

, many of which use complex device geometries depending on their intended
 application.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "jariwala2016mixeddimensional"

\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The majority of van der Waals' heterostructures use vertical transport of
 charge carriers between layers.
 However there are are also lateral heterostructures which use planar transport
 across grain boundaries between different 2D materials.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "park2014spatially,zhang2015synthesis,gong2014vertical"

\end_inset

 They are comparitively complex to synthesise, requiring carefully controlled
 growth conditions and have similar constraints to traditional semiconductor
 heterostructures.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "bogaert2016diffusionmediated"

\end_inset

 They are not discussed further in this thesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Vertical van der Waals' heterostructures mostly benefit from a particularly
 humble mechanism: carrier tunneling through hBN.
 Carrier injection into early van der Waals' heterostructure devices was
 usually carried out by adding gold contacts to the surface or edges of
 graphene.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "wang2013onedimensional,kretinin2014electronic"

\end_inset

 This technique works reasonably well, however the contact resistance and
 damage to the graphene is difficult to control.
 It was later found that carriers could be injected into the heterostructure
 through an insulating layer of hBN.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lee2011electron"

\end_inset

 This came with some advantages as the thickness of the hBN determined the
 tunneling resistance of the contact
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "britnell2012electron"

\end_inset

, the buried conductive sheets were protected from the gold contact by the
 hBN, and the surfaces self-cleaned
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "withers2015lightemitting"

\end_inset

.
 Furthermore, quantum tunneling through hBN preserves the spin state of
 the carriers allowing spin transport in graphene to be explored using ferromagn
etic contacts.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "kamalakar2014enhanced,chandni2015evidence"

\end_inset

 hBN also allows different components to be isolated from one another until
 high enough potential enduces quantum tunneling of carriers between components.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lee2011electron,britnell2012electron"

\end_inset

 This has lead to some of the highest on/off ratios in 2D material devices
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "yang2012graphene,georgiou2013vertical,roy2014fieldeffect,britnell2012fieldeffect"

\end_inset

 as well as allowing the electronic properties of unstable materials to
 be probed
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "cao2015quality,mishchenko2015nonlocal"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Van der Waals' heterostructures are not limited to layering different 2D
 materials; stacking materials of varying dimensional confinement is also
 of great interest to electronics and nanofluidics thanks to the fine control
 and cleanliness of these structures.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "chamberlain2012sizestructure,huang2015superior,jariwala2016mixeddimensional,algara-siller2015squareice"

\end_inset

 Fundamental experimental work on water flow phenomena in nanoscale capilaries
 using a graphite-based heterostructure is presented in Chapter 5.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Finally, a surprising application of van der Waals' heterostructures is
 in TEM imaging of 2D materials.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "zan2013control,algara-siller2013thepristine"

\end_inset

 By encapsulating a 2D material in graphene the enclosed material can be
 studied with higher doses of electrons without structural damage.
 Higher doses of electrons increase the signal to noise ratio when acquiring
 an image or spectroscopic data.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lee2014electron"

\end_inset

 Previous efforts to image 2D materials were limited by structural damage
 and rapid formation of defects under the electron beam.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand citep
key "egerton2010basicquestions,ramasse2012directexperimental,garcia2014analysis,komsa2013frompoint"

\end_inset

 Graphene provides rapid charge transfer away from the irradiated site,
 limiting radiolysis effects, and also protects the 2D material from mobile
 contaminants that can act as etchants.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "zan2013control,algara-siller2013thepristine"

\end_inset

 This is a highly successful method for imaging 2D materials, however filtering
 of the fourier transform of both graphene lattices is required to isolate
 the structure of interest.This results in the potential loss of useful informati
on about the target lattice and often image simulation is required to interpret
 the data.
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lehtinen2014noninvasive"

\end_inset

 Nevertheless, this method has been applied to many different 2D materials
 to study their intrinsic defects
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "lin2016defectengineering"

\end_inset

[Lan's paper?] and most recently 
\emph on
in situ
\emph default
 microscopy of liquid systems
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "algara-siller2015squareice,vasu2016vander"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Characterisation of van der Waals' heterostructures
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
UNFINISHED
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary method for characterising a van der Waals' heterostructure the
 electronic transport measurements or the measurement of a physical phenomenon.
 For the most part this is due to van der Waals' heterostructures being
 relatively new and so standards in quality have not been established.
 Characterisation by this method relies on the sensitivity of the phenomenon
 being measured, for example simple electron mobility measurements are less
 sensitive to structural defects and impurites than charge density wave
 propagation.
 For now any evidence of a device working satisfies the burdon of proof
 for its purpose (i.e.
 publication).
 This is beginning to change as the requirement for device reproducibility
 and standards comes into demand.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Common characterisation techniques for probing device structure and quality
 are atomic force microscopy (AFM), scanning near-field optical microscopy
 (SNOM), and Raman spectroscopy.
 These are often used not only on heterostructures but on the isolated 2D
 material flakes prior to assembly.
\end_layout

